ORGO.reg({"types":{"nuc-pairs":{"id":"nuc-pairs","title":"Circle the stronger nucleophile","exam":"Q1C","points":15,"asks":"Two species are shown. Circle the one that attacks carbon faster in an {{SN2}} reaction.","know":[{"h":"<b>Nucleophilicity is a rate</b>: how fast a species displaces a leaving group in an {{SN2}} reaction. Basicity is an equilibrium ({{pKa}} of the conjugate acid). They often agree, but not always.","from":"Lecture 11/05, p. 2"},{"h":"<b>The lecture table</b> (relative {{SN2}} rates): {{HC≡C-}} 126,000; {{HS-}} 126,000; {{I-}} 80,000; {{HO-}} 16,000; {{N3-}} 8,000; {{NH3}} 8,000; {{Cl-}} 1,000; {{CH3CO2-}} 630; {{CH3OH}} 1; {{H2O}} 1.","from":"Table 7.5"},{"h":"<b>Across a row</b>, nucleophilicity follows basicity: it increases to the left ({{H2N-}} > {{HO-}} > {{F-}}).","from":"Lecture 11/05, p. 3"},{"h":"<b>Down a column</b>, nucleophilicity increases while basicity decreases ({{I-}} > {{Br-}} > {{Cl-}}; {{HS-}} > {{HO-}}): larger atoms are more polarizable and less tightly solvated.","from":"Lecture 11/05, p. 3"},{"h":"<b>Charge</b>: an anion is a better nucleophile than its neutral conjugate acid ({{HO-}} 16,000 against {{H2O}} 1).","from":"Table 7.5"},{"h":"<b>Bulk</b>: with the same attacking atom, the bulkier species is the poorer nucleophile. tert-Butoxide is a strong base but a poor nucleophile.","from":"Lecture 11/05, p. 2 to 3"},{"h":"<b>Resonance</b>: a charge spread over several atoms is less available. Acetate (630) is far weaker than hydroxide (16,000).","from":"Table 7.5"}],"method":["Find the atom that attacks in each species.","Same atom? The one with the negative charge wins; if both are charged, the less bulky and less delocalized one wins.","Different atoms in the same row? The one further left (the stronger base) wins.","Different atoms in the same column? The lower one wins, although it is the weaker base.","Check your answer against the lecture table if both species are on it."],"pitfalls":[{"h":"Choosing the stronger base when the atoms are in the same column (choosing {{Cl-}} over {{I-}}, or {{HO-}} over {{HS-}})."},{"h":"Choosing tert-butoxide because it is the stronger base: bulk makes it the weaker nucleophile."}],"links":{"read":["sn2-2"],"lab":["nucleophiles"]}}},"items":{"exam-q1c-i":{"id":"exam-q1c-i","type":"nuc-pairs","role":"exam","kind":"mcq","diff":1,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q1C(i)","layout":"pair","stem":"Circle the stronger nucleophile.","choices":[{"h":"[[mol:[I-]]]","ok":true,"why":"Iodide is lower in the column: nucleophilicity increases going down. Lecture table: 80,000."},{"h":"[[mol:[Cl-]]]","ok":false,"why":"This ranks by basicity. Chloride is the stronger base, but nucleophilicity is a rate of attack on carbon, and in the lecture table chloride is 1,000 against 80,000 for iodide."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Find the attacking atoms","h":"I and Cl: same charge, same column of the periodic table."},{"t":"Use the column trend","h":"The lecture's sketch: basicity increases to the left and up, nucleophilicity increases to the left and <b>down</b>. In a column the two trends run in opposite directions."},{"t":"Check against the table","h":"Table 7.5: {{I-}} 80,000, {{Cl-}} 1,000."}],"answer":"{{I-}}","rule":"Down a column: weaker base, better nucleophile."}},"exam-q1c-ii":{"id":"exam-q1c-ii","type":"nuc-pairs","role":"exam","kind":"mcq","diff":1,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q1C(ii)","layout":"pair","stem":"Circle the stronger nucleophile.","choices":[{"h":"[[mol:[SH-]]]","ok":true,"why":"Sulfur is below oxygen in the same column: the better nucleophile. Lecture table: 126,000."},{"h":"[[mol:[OH-]]]","ok":false,"why":"This ranks by basicity. Hydroxide is the stronger base ({{H2O}} {{pKa}} 15.7 against {{H2S}} 10), but it is the slower nucleophile: 16,000 in the lecture table."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Find the attacking atoms","h":"S and O: same charge, same column."},{"t":"Use the column trend","h":"Nucleophilicity increases down a column. Basicity runs the other way: {{HO-}} is the stronger base, {{HS-}} the stronger nucleophile."},{"t":"Check against the table","h":"Table 7.5: {{HS-}} 126,000, {{HO-}} 16,000."}],"answer":"{{HS-}}","rule":"Same column: the lower atom is the better nucleophile."}},"exam-q1c-iii":{"id":"exam-q1c-iii","type":"nuc-pairs","role":"exam","kind":"mcq","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q1C(iii)","layout":"pair","stem":"Circle the stronger nucleophile.","choices":[{"h":"[[mol:CC(C)(C)[O-]]]","ok":false,"why":"tert-Butoxide is the stronger base (tert-butanol {{pKa}} 17.0 against methanol 15.5), but three methyl groups crowd the oxygen. The lecture calls it a poor nucleophile."},{"h":"[[mol:C[O-]]]","ok":true,"why":"Same attacking atom, same charge, much less bulk: methoxide reaches carbon far more easily."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Find the attacking atoms","h":"Oxygen in both, with the same negative charge. The periodic trends cannot decide this one."},{"t":"Compare the size around the atom","h":"The lecture's rule: a bulkier nucleophile is less nucleophilic. To attack carbon the oxygen must get close to the back of the electrophile, and the three methyl groups of tert-butoxide get in the way."},{"t":"Do not let basicity mislead you","h":"tert-Butoxide is the stronger base, because taking a small, exposed proton is not hindered. Stronger base, weaker nucleophile."}],"answer":"{{CH3O-}}","rule":"Same atom and charge: the less hindered one is the better nucleophile."}},"nuc-pairs-s01":{"id":"nuc-pairs-s01","type":"nuc-pairs","role":"worked","kind":"mcq","diff":1,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q1C(i)","layout":"pair","stem":"Circle the stronger nucleophile.","choices":[{"h":"[[mol:[I-]]]","ok":true,"why":"Iodide is lower in the column: larger, more polarizable, and held less tightly by a protic solvent. Lecture table: 80,000."},{"h":"[[mol:[Cl-]]]","ok":false,"why":"Chloride is the stronger base but the weaker nucleophile. Lecture table: 1,000, eighty times slower than iodide."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Find the attacking atoms","h":"I and Cl. Same charge, same column of the periodic table.","ask":{"q":"Same row or same column?","a":"Same column (the halogens)."}},{"t":"Use the column trend","h":"Down a column nucleophilicity <b>increases</b>, even though basicity decreases. The bigger atom's electrons are easier to push toward carbon, and a protic solvent solvates the small ion more tightly.","ask":{"q":"Which way does basicity run in a column?","a":"Up: chloride is the stronger base. Nucleophilicity runs the other way."}},{"t":"Check against the table","h":"{{I-}} 80,000 against {{Cl-}} 1,000."}],"answer":"{{I-}}","rule":"Down a column: bigger atom, better nucleophile."}}},"mols":{"[I-]":{"w":14,"h":11,"p":[],"t":[[0,1.8,9.3,10,"I"],[0,7.6,5.3,6,"-"]],"a":[[4.3,4.3,"I",-1,4,0,0]],"b":[]},"[Cl-]":{"w":16,"h":11,"p":[],"t":[[0,2.5,9.3,10,"C"],[0,9.4,9.3,10,"l"],[0,11.5,5.3,6,"-"]],"a":[[10.3,4.3,"Cl",-1,4,0,0]],"b":[]},"[SH-]":{"w":22,"h":11,"p":[],"t":[[0,3.1,9.3,10,"H"],[0,10,9.3,10,"S"],[0,16.4,5.3,6,"-"]],"a":[[12.8,4.3,"S",-1,3,1,0]],"b":[]},"[OH-]":{"w":22,"h":11,"p":[],"t":[[0,3.1,9.3,10,"H"],[0,9.5,9.3,10,"O"],[0,16.4,5.3,6,"-"]],"a":[[12.5,4.3,"O",-1,3,1,0]],"b":[]},"CC(C)(C)[O-]":{"w":64,"h":68,"p":[[0,"M 7.8,49.1 L 31.7,35.3",0],[1,"M 31.7,35.3 L 55.5,21.5",0],[2,"M 31.7,35.3 L 45.4,59.2",0],[3,"M 31.7,35.3 L 21.6,17.9",0]],"t":[[4,14.6,17,11,"O"],[4,22.2,12.6,7,"-"]],"a":[[7.8,49.1,"C",0,0,3,0],[31.7,35.3,"C",0,0,0,0],[55.5,21.5,"C",0,0,3,0],[45.4,59.2,"C",0,0,3,0],[17.9,11.5,"O",-1,3,0,0]],"b":[[0,1,1],[1,2,1],[1,3,1],[1,4,1]]},"C[O-]":{"w":45,"h":11,"p":[[0,"M 7.1,4.3 L 28.3,4.3",0]],"t":[[1,29.1,9.3,10,"O"],[1,36,5.3,6,"-"]],"a":[[7.1,4.3,"C",0,0,3,0],[32.1,4.3,"O",-1,3,0,0]],"b":[[0,1,1]]}}});
